A||||1||Carrolles||R134a conversion
Z||000000||Carrolles||04-19-99||11:19 AM||||I am about to replace the locked up A/C compressor in my 85SE 2m6 4spd.  Has anyone converted to R-134a  successfully in a Fiero?  I had my 89 Bonneville converted about two years ago and have had no trouble with it so far.<br>Thanks<br>Carroll<br>
Z||000001||theogre||04-19-99||12:06 PM||||I've seen a number of things around the web that indicate you can do this. With a few rules. Replace the compressor, and reciever/dryer/accumulator. Replace ALL seals in the system. Preasure test the system for at least a couple days to make sure there are no slow leaks.<p>Even then there is no guarantee you won't have a problem. R134 operates at much higher presure than R12. This tends to really stress the old parts. Your 89 was built just as all this stuff hit the fan, but the Fiero is signifigantly older in freon years.<br>
